Very interesting, that the cockatrice has been appearing since Hack
1.0, and yet there still seems to be no explanation as to why these
lizards work they way they do. Although I am inclined to believe that
it was simply creative license taken by the devteam, I nevertheless
decided to look into the history of the basilisk / cockatrice mythos.
Although I perused many sources on the internet, I wasn't able to find
any literature directly relating to the subject at hand. Based on most
of what i've read from the D&D monster manual, historical sources, etc,
cockatrices are supposed to have an appearance that can be described as
a rooster with lizard-like or reptilian characteristics. Furthermore,
they are supposed to be born of a chicken's egg that has been incubated
by a frog, toad, or snake.

Based on this information, I can only surmise that, considering
cockatrices are partly reptile, perhaps other reptiles were considered
to be less susceptable to the petrifying affectations that their
mythological brethren can cause. Although i've not been able to find
any literature to back this up, it seems to be somewhat logical. Ex:
Some species of snakes are immune, or at least resistant to their own
venom.

On a somewhat related note, i'd be interested to find out whether live
lizards in nethack are susceptable to petrification, whether it be by
rubber-chickening, or having a tame one for a companion. I, for one,
would venture to guess that they can in fact be stoned.



